[FINAL-TEXT] This is definitive. The complete picture is now clear:

- **mswitch0** owns three VLAN SVIs: `vlan10@172.16.10.254/24`, `vlan20@172.16.20.254/24`, `vlan30@172.16.30.254/24` — the gateways for all Campus 1 PCs (PC5–PC14)
- **mswitch0's FRR running config has NO `router ospf` section whatsoever** — OSPF is completely absent
- mswitch0's kernel routing table shows only its 4 directly connected prefixes (10.2.2.0/24 + three 172.16.x.x VLANs) — no default route, no OSPF-learned routes
- As a result, the three 172.16.x.x subnets are **never originated into the OSPF domain**; no other router has a route to them
- PC5–PC14 can ping each other only because mswitch0 does local inter-VLAN routing — no external routing needed
- The on-call engineer's observation that "router11 lacks Campus 1 prefixes" is correct, but router11 is healthy — it simply never receives those routes because mswitch0 never advertises them

The fault is confirmed: **`ospf_neighbor_missing` on mswitch0** — the entire OSPF configuration block is missing from the multilayer switch that owns the 172.16.x.x campus networks.

