is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: mswitch0
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The FRR/OSPF configuration on mswitch0 (the Campus 1 multilayer switch). The `router ospf` stanza is entirely absent from mswitch0's FRR running configuration — there are no `network` statements and no OSPF adjacency is formed.

**Mechanism:** mswitch0 owns three VLAN SVI interfaces that serve as the default gateways for all Campus 1 PCs: `vlan10` at 172.16.10.254/24 (PC5–PC8), `vlan20` at 172.16.20.254/24 (PC9–PC11), and `vlan30` at 172.16.30.254/24 (PC12–PC14). It also holds the 10.2.2.2/24 uplink to router16. Because mswitch0 runs no OSPF, it advertises none of these prefixes into the routing domain. Every other router's OSPF table is therefore missing all 172.16.x.x entries — confirmed on router17 (gateway for the 10.5.0.x/10.6.0.x campus) and router16 itself. Intra-campus pings (172.16.x.x ↔ 172.16.x.x) still succeed because mswitch0 routes them locally across its VLAN interfaces without needing OSPF. Cross-campus pings from 10.5.0.x/10.6.0.x to 172.16.x.x time out because sshswitch and router17 have no forwarding entry for those destinations.

**Localization:** mswitch0, FRR daemon — missing `router ospf` configuration block entirely.

**Root cause:** The OSPF configuration was removed (or never applied) on mswitch0, causing the 172.16.10.0/24, 172.16.20.0/24, and 172.16.30.0/24 subnets to be unreachable from the rest of the OSPF domain. Router11 is fully healthy with correct adjacencies; restarting it would have no effect.

**Fix:** Add an `router ospf` block to mswitch0's FRR config with `network 10.2.2.0/24 area 0`, `network 172.16.10.0/24 area 0`, `network 172.16.20.0/24 area 0`, and `network 172.16.30.0/24 area 0`, then reload FRR (`vtysh -c 'write'` and `service frr reload`).
=== END ===